  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nUpper low pressure remains in the Central Plains with a trough\nstretching down into New Mexico. This low and trough are on a\nweakening trend and will be folded into the synoptic flow across\nthe Ohio River Valley today. Moisture streams northeastward from\nTexas to the Ohio River Valley bringing widespread showers to\nthe Quad State through the morning, continuing through the\nafternoon in Western Kentucky. Instability remains limited\nkeeping severe weather at bay, with even lightning activity\nsparse during the day. QPF is around 0.75-1.5in which, spread \nout over a dozen hours, shouldn't produce any flooding issues. \nThe moisture plume is directed through Tennessee Saturday and \nmight result in a few showers in the Southern Pennyrile Sunday, \nbut PoPs have trended lower.\n\nThe cold frontal passage brings in much cooler air, with highs\nonly in the 70s today. High pressure drifts through the Great\nLakes maintains northeasterly flow through the weekend which \nkeeps highs in the 70s and lows in the 50s. A second surge of \ncold air early week as another high pressure area moves into the\nMidwest drops lows to the 40s Monday night and Tuesday night. \nSome warming is likely late in the week as a more southerly flow\ndevelops. Models are strongly aligned on dry conditions Sunday \nthrough the rest of the 7 day forecast period.",
